{"id":"b26b6ae1-9e7d-4981-97d1-bf73702db8a5","object":"chat.completion","created":1793334100,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"\n\n```python\nprint(search_code(\"NewLineFormatter(\", [\"deepinfer\"], 20))\nprint(search_code(\"logger.info(m)\", [\"deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py\"], 10))\nprint(search_code(\"from deepinfer.environ\", [\"deepinfer/logger.py\"], 10))\n```\n\nNow I'll implement the config in environ.py.\n\n```python\nr = read_file(\"deepinfer/environ.py\")\nh = r[\"file_hash\"]\nnew = '''\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value} is less than the minimum allowed value {minimum}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n'''\nold = '''\n    @staticmethod\n    def choice('''\nres = replace_text(\"deepinfer/environ.py\", old, new + old, h)\nprint(res)\n```\n\n```python\nr = read_file(\"deepinfer/environ.py\")\nh = r[\"file_hash\"]\nold = '''    DEEPINFER_LOGGING_INTERVAL_STEP = EnvVar.int(default=100)'''\nnew = '''    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)\n    \"普通诊断日志消息正文的最大字符数；未设置表示不限长，设置时须为不小于32的整数。\"\n    DEEPINFER_LOGGING_INTERVAL_STEP = EnvVar.int(default=100)'''\nprint(replace_text(\"deepinfer/environ.py\", old, new, h))\n```","reasoning_content":"I've read all three files and baseline is done. Now I need to check the logger.py file's _setup_logger and formatter; already have. I need to also confirm imports in logger.py for environ usage.\n\nLet me implement the edits. First environ.py: add a factory method `int_or_none_min` or a custom descriptor for DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS.\n\nRequirement: unset -> None; set -> int parse, >=32; empty string, unparseable, or <32 -> ValueError containing variable name. Note that empty string: os.getenv returns \"\" only if set to empty; EnvVar passes \"\" -> int(\"\") raises ValueError, which gets wrapped with name prefix. Good.\n\nAdd factory method:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_at_least(minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value} is less than the minimum {minimum}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nAnd in Logging section: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_at_least(32)`.\n\nNote: \"空串\" — int(\"\") raises ValueError with name prefix. Fine.\n\nNow logger.py:\n\n```python\n_MESSAGE_OMITTED = \"...<omitted>...\"\n```\n\nNewLineFormatter.__init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\nvalidate: if max_message_chars is not None: if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32: raise ValueError(...).\n\nformat(): \n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            msg = self._truncate(record, msg)\n        ...\n```\n\nCareful: budget only limits message body, not prefix, exc/stack info. So truncation should be applied to record.message before super().format? But we must not modify record. Approach: compute message = record.getMessage(), truncate it, then format using a copy? Simpler: temporarily... no, cannot modify record.\n\nBetter: replicate formatting? Alternative: create a shallow copy of record with truncated msg and args=(). Using copy.copy(record) doesn't touch original. But spec says \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args...\" — copying is fine.\n\nSimplest: build a copy:\n```python\nimport copy\n...\ntarget = record\nif truncation needed:\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    if len(message) > budget:\n        target = copy.copy(record)\n        target.msg = truncated\n        target.args = ()\n        ...\n```\nBut copy.copy of LogRecord copies __dict__? LogRecord has __dict__, so copy.copy works (shallow copy of instance dict). However record.message attribute may be set by earlier formatter call? super().format sets record.message = record.getMessage(). Since the same record goes to multiple handlers, the first handler's format sets record.message. If we truncate on a copy, original message remains full. Good.\n\nActually careful: `super().format(record)` sets record.message on the original record. Then the second handler formats — record.message already set but super().format recomputes record.message = record.getMessage() so it's fine.\n\nSo:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        if (\n            self.max_message_chars is not None\n            and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True\n        ):\n            budget = self.max_message_chars\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            if len(message) > budget:\n                record = copy.copy(record)\n                record.msg = _truncate_message(message, budget)\n                record.args = ()\n                record.message = record.msg\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, wait: does copy.copy on LogRecord work? Create a small test later. Also `record.exc_text` caching on the copy: fine.\n\nBut careful: `copy.copy(record)` — LogRecord defines __reduce__? Actually logging.LogRecord has no __copy__; copy.copy copies via __reduce_ex__ which uses __class__ and __dict__ ... LogRecord has no __slots__, so it's created via object.__reduce_ex__(4) → `copyreg.__newobj__`, calling cls.__new__(cls). Should be fine. Alternatively manually: `record = logging.LogRecord(...)` complex. Let's test with copy.copy.\n\nHmm, one risk: `logging.Logger.makeRecord` sets attributes; copy keeps them. Fine.\n\nAlternatively avoid copy: format without using record.msg by constructing via `self.formatMessage`? We could do:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        ... truncated\n        # then temporarily set record.message? \n```\nApproach: temporarily swap record.msg/args, call super().format, restore in finally. That mutates the record briefly but with finally restore; but spec says don't rewrite shared record. A copy is cleaner. Let's use copy with a try/except fallback? Keep simple; test.\n\nTruncation function:\n```python\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, budget: int) -> str:\n    remaining = budget - len(_MESSAGE_OMITTED)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return f\"{message[:head]}{_MESSAGE_OMITTED}{message[len(message) - tail:] if tail else ''}\"\n```\nCareful when tail == 0 -> message[-0:] is whole string. Use conditional.\n\nBudget 32: remaining = 32-15 = 17, head = 9, tail = 8. Matches example.\n\nAlso new validation for max_message_chars: must be int >= 32, bool not int.\n\n_setup_logger:\n```python\n    fmt = NewLineFormatter(\n        _FORMAT,\n        datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT,\n        use_color=sys.stdout.isatty(),\n        max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS,\n    )\n```\nNote: _setup_logger is called at import; envs cached. Re-initialization: tests probably call logger._setup_logger() again after rearm. Import of environ in logger: need `from deepinfer.environ import envs` — check for circular imports: environ imports os, threading, datetime only. So fine.\n\nstats_logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nBut wait: logger.info with extra attribute — the same record object flows to handlers. Fine. But careful: if the message is a string and args... fine.\n\nNow let me check logger.py imports has `copy`? No. Add `import copy`.\n\nAlso check lo